<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Härjedalen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Härjedalen is a mountainous province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Long ruled from Norway, it passed with Jämtland to Sweden under the Treaty of Brömsebro in 1645, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torstenson-war">Torstenson War</a>. Sparsely populated, it is known for its forests and fells and for reindeer herding by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a>; its largest town is Sveg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harjedalen.jpg" alt="Härjedalen" width="320" height="729" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lapplänning (highlighting by Lokal_Profil) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverigekarta-Landskap_H%C3%A4rjedalen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Torstenson War of 1643–1645 Swedish armies overran Jutland, and by the Treaty of Brömsebro Denmark-Norway ceded Gotland, Ösel and the Norwegian provinces of Jämtland and <b>Härjedalen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Denmark lost the Torstenson War against Sweden, and by the Treaty of Brömsebro in 1645 it ceded Ösel to Sweden, together with Gotland, Jämtland and <b>Härjedalen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-osel">Danish Ösel</a></li></ul></section>
